\subsection{Prescriptive Results}\label{sec:prescriptive}

\paragraph{Entity-level construction.}
We aggregate entities across samples, retaining those exceeding $\theta{=}2/N$, with LP-weighted or uniform scoring ($\theta$ stable in $[0.15, 0.35]$; Appendix~\ref{app:theta_sweep}). In zero-shot, LP-weighting is necessary (SciERC $+1.5$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale}).

LP-weighted construction improves on Few-NERD ($+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp, $p{<}0.001$; Holm $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.006$, $k{=}10$; Table~\ref{tab:entity_construction}); SciERC shows a smaller, non-significant gain ($+0.32$\,pp, 3-seed); CoNLL shows no gain ($-0.39$\,pp), consistent with high degeneracy.
Gains require both low degeneracy \textit{and} sufficient oracle headroom (Few-NERD: $D{=}13$\%, headroom${=}12.4$\,pp).\footnote{Holm--Bonferroni \cite{holm1979simple} within families ($k{=}10$, $21$, $9$); global $k{=}57$: $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.055$ (Appendix~\ref{app:holm}).}

\paragraph{Complementary strategies.}
Construction and LP selection are complementary, associated with within-instance LP alignment $\rho_{\text{adj}}$ (Few-NERD $0.516$ favors both; SciERC $0.167$ favors construction only).
QE signals also support selective prediction: at 80\% coverage, filtering yields $+5$--$8$\,pp F1 (AUPRC 0.70--0.92; Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).

